window.docContents[167].resolve({"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-counting-a-set-set-ncard-and-set-encard":{"contents":"Finally these two ways of counting types extend to sets.\n\n* Set.ncard s : ℕ is the size as a natural number, 0 if s is infinite. It is\n  Nat.card of the corresponding subtype.* Set.encard s : ℕ∞ is the size in ℕ∞, ⊤ if s is infinite (the Set analogue\n  of ENat.card).\n\n#check Set.ncard  -- {α : Type u_1} → Set α → ℕ\n#check Set.encard -- {α : Type u_1} → Set α → ℕ∞\n#check Nat.card_coe_set_eq -- (s : Set α) : Nat.card ↑s = s.ncard\n\n\nWhen the set is the coercion of a Finset, ncard is just that Finset's card.\n\nexample {V : Type*} (s : Finset V) : (s : Set V).ncard = s.card :=\n  Set.ncard_coe_finset _\n\n\nSo for a finite graph the (noncomputable) G.edgeSet.ncard and the (computable)\nG.edgeFinset.card agree.\n\nexample {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) [Fintype G.edgeSet] :\n    G.edgeSet.ncard = G.edgeFinset.card := by\n  rw [← coe_edgeFinset, Set.ncard_coe_finset]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Finite Types\u0009Cardinality","header":"4.2.4.6. Counting a  Set :  Set.ncard  and  Set.encard","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-counting-a-set-set-ncard-and-set-encard"}});